Ansible Delegation & Parallelism

Today I learned about Delegation & Parallelism in Ansible, which help control where tasks run and how fast they execute ⚡
In large infrastructures, running tasks efficiently and on the right nodes is very important.
 This is where delegation and parallel execution become powerful 🚀

🔹 🎯 delegate_to
The delegate_to keyword is used to run a task on a different host instead of the target host.
Example use cases:
 • Run DB tasks from control node
 • Load balancer updates
 • Centralized operations
This allows better control over task execution location 🌐

🔹 🔁 run_once
The run_once: true keyword ensures a task runs only once, even if multiple hosts are targeted.
Useful when:
 • Downloading a file once
 • Running migrations
 • Performing one-time setup tasks
It avoids duplicate execution across multiple servers.

🔹 📊 Serial Execution
serial controls how many hosts run tasks at a time.
Example:
 serial: 2 → Runs tasks on 2 hosts at a time
This is helpful for:
 • Rolling updates
 • Zero-downtime deployments
 • Controlled automation
It reduces risk during large-scale changes 🔄

🔹 🚀 forks (Parallelism)
forks defines how many hosts Ansible can manage in parallel.
Higher forks = faster execution ⚡
 Lower forks = controlled execution
It is configured in the Ansible configuration file and is very useful in large environments with many servers.

🔥 3-Line Takeaway
Delegation controls where tasks run.
 run_once prevents duplicate executions.
 Parallelism improves speed and scalability.
